# If a user would feel it, fix it

This is a **must-do** rule (Josh, November 7).

Any difference, gap or limitation that an actual user would feel gets fixed.
"It only matters while casting", "it's rare" or "Chrome does the same" are
not reasons to leave it. Casting was the example: leaving a page restarted a
cast, and an agent proposed to leave it because it "only matters while
casting". Casting is a core experience, not an edge case.

Something a user feels includes: playback restarting, stopping, jumping back,
freezing, going blank, rebuffering, stuttering or losing sync; sound starting,
stopping or missing; a video that won't play; controls (Now Playing, media
keys, picture in picture, casting, fullscreen) doing the wrong thing; slower
starts; noticeably worse battery or heat.

- Before proposing to leave anything, ask: would someone using Parc notice
  this while watching, listening, casting, using picture in picture, switching
  tabs or apps, or navigating? If yes, it's a fix, however large. Say how big
  it is and start it.
- Only differences no user could perceive (a page-internal ordering of events
  within one task, a timestamp that differs by microseconds) may be proposed
  as "leave", and those still go to Josh before they go into any document.
- When matching Safari would make the experience worse (a video that plays
  today would stop playing), say so plainly and recommend the better
  experience; that is Josh's call.

## Real usage only (Josh, November 8)

"Would feel it" means in realistic use, like Josh's own (a sidebar of about
five folders and 20 to 100 items), not at stress-test sizes. A harness's large
sizes are for spotting scaling bugs, never a reason to start new work. On
November 8 a stutter report grew into four hours of work on 200-folder
sidebars nobody has. Fix what's felt at real sizes, confirm it, and stop.

## How to report a difference

Report every difference as what the user experiences, not what the code does:
the everyday situation, what they see or hear, and how often they'd hit it.
Answer the obvious follow-ups before Josh has to ask them: does it affect
picture in picture, casting, fullscreen, background tabs, Now Playing and
media keys, protected (FairPlay) video; does keeping the current behaviour
break sites that expect Safari's; and what fixing it costs.
